proposed_comments.body:
### Preserve hash characters in executable filters

**Severity:** `medium`

`CleanScalarSpan` currently treats every `#` as a comment, including a hash inside a quoted value or an unquoted executable name such as `app#1.exe`. Those legal names are silently truncated in the generated filter, so the matching application does not receive its shortcuts.

Recognize comments only outside a leading quoted scalar and after whitespace (or at the start). Apostrophes inside plain values must remain ordinary characters. This retains the existing quoted-escape-decoding limitation; it does not add YAML escape decoding.

```suggestion
            bool inSingleQuotes = !span.IsEmpty && span[0] == '\'';
            bool inDoubleQuotes = !span.IsEmpty && span[0] == '"';
            for (int i = inSingleQuotes || inDoubleQuotes ? 1 : 0; i < span.Length; i++)
            {
                if (inDoubleQuotes && span[i] == '\\')
                {
                    i++;
                    continue;
                }

                if (inSingleQuotes && span[i] == '\'')
                {
                    if (i + 1 < span.Length && span[i + 1] == '\'')
                    {
                        i++;
                        continue;
                    }

                    inSingleQuotes = false;
                    continue;
                }

                if (inDoubleQuotes && span[i] == '"')
                {
                    inDoubleQuotes = false;
                    continue;
                }

                if (span[i] == '#' &&
                    !inSingleQuotes &&
                    !inDoubleQuotes &&
                    (i == 0 || char.IsWhiteSpace(span[i - 1])))
                {
                    span = span[..i].TrimEnd();
                    break;
                }
            }
```
